**The `consume()` fix.** There was no issue or PR tracking it, so I opened #65796. One correction to what I wrote earlier: I said that fix belonged on a lower branch. Looking at the history, consuming all limiters on a rejected hit was a deliberate choice (#38353, to keep the result independent from limiter order), and the security login throttler relies on the same semantics in its own loop. Reverting a deliberate behavior is a behavior change, not a bugfix, so #65796 targets 8.2 and this PR should land right after it. I verified the pair end to end: with a per-user limit of 1 and a shared quota of 10, a client hammering 20 requests gets 1 through, and the next honest user is still accepted. On this branch alone, the same run exhausts the shared window and locks the honest user out, which is why the merge order matters.

**`reset()`.** Documenting the behavior as-is, no exemption. Your analysis is right: exempting fixed-key sub-limiters means threading that knowledge into `CompoundLimiter`, which is public API carried for the whole major, for a case that is better served by documentation. And an exemption would leave no way at all to clear the shared window through the compound, while the current behavior at least keeps every window reachable. Anyone needing a narrower reset can reset the sub-limiters through their own factories. I added the points to carry to the docs at the end of the description.

